  "narration": {
    "pattern_romaji": "A wa B desu / A wa B ja nai desu",
    "pattern_jp": "AはBです／AはBじゃないです",
    "pattern_english": "A is B / A isn't B (B = a na-adjective)",
    "slots": [
      {
        "slot": "A",
        "fits": "the topic: a person, thing or place (おかあさん, 私のかぞく, このへや), often built with の or この"
      },
      {
        "slot": "B",
        "fits": "a な-adjective describing A: きれい (pretty / clean), げんき (energetic, well), しずか (quiet), ゆうめい (famous), ふあんてい (unstable). Used plainly, with no な before です"
      }
    ],
    "segments": [
      {
        "kind": "en",
        "speaker": "A",
        "speed": "teaching",
        "text": "Welcome back! Today's pattern lets you say what something is like, using a new family of describing words. Here it is."
      },
      {
        "kind": "jp",
        "speaker": "A",
        "speed": "teaching",
        "text": "AはBです"
      },
      {
        "kind": "en",
        "speaker": "A",
        "speed": "teaching",
        "text": "It means: A is B. Here, B is a describing word called a {な}-adjective. The negative is this:"
      },
      {
        "kind": "jp",
        "speaker": "A",
        "speed": "teaching",
        "text": "AはBじゃないです"
      },
      {
        "kind": "en",
        "speaker": "A",
        "speed": "teaching",
        "text": "A is not B. So: A is pretty, or A isn't quiet."
      },
      {
        "kind": "en",
        "speaker": "A",
        "speed": "teaching",
        "text": "Let's go through the slots. A is the topic, the person or thing you're talking about. Next comes {は}, the topic marker you already know."
      },
      {
        "kind": "en",
        "speaker": "A",
        "speed": "teaching",
        "text": "Slot B is the {な}-adjective. Three of them come up in today's dialogue: {きれい}, which means pretty or clean, {げんき}, which means energetic or well, and {しずか}, which means quiet."
      },
      {
        "kind": "en",
        "speaker": "A",
        "speed": "teaching",
        "text": "Then {です} comes last, just like with nouns. The word order is topic, then description, then {です}. In English, the word is comes in the middle. In Japanese, it comes at the very end."
      },
      {
        "kind": "en",
        "speaker": "A",
        "speed": "teaching",
        "text": "Let's build one. Start with the topic: mother."
      },
      {
        "kind": "jp",
        "speaker": "B",
        "speed": "teaching",
        "text": "おかあさん"
      },
      {
        "kind": "en",
        "speaker": "A",
        "speed": "teaching",
        "text": "Add the topic marker."
      },
      {
        "kind": "jp",
        "speaker": "B",
        "speed": "teaching",
        "text": "おかあさんは"
      },
